<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Reuss-Schleiz</h1><p class="st-meta">1647 CE – 1848 CE · capital Schleiz</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Branch of the Reuss family in Thuringia that eventually reunited the Younger Line of Reuss under its rule.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Reuss-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleiz">Schleiz</a> was created in the 1647 division of the Younger Line of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-reuss">House of Reuss</a>, centred on the town of Schleiz. The counts, descended from the medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vogt">Vögte</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weida">Weida</a>, Gera and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plauen">Plauen</a>, held small territories in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The territory was small and agrarian, with textile crafts, and its counts, all named Heinrich, ruled through a Lutheran church and a small court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleiz">Schleiz</a>. The Schleiz line was raised to princely rank in 1806, joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a> and then the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/german-confederation">German Confederation</a>, and shared the inheritance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/principality-of-reuss-younger-line">Reuss-Gera</a> when that line died out in 1802.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1848, after the abdication of the Prince of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-reuss-ebersdorf">Reuss-Lobenstein-Ebersdorf</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleiz">Schleiz</a> line united all lands of the Younger Line as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/principality-of-reuss-younger-line">Principality of Reuss Younger Line</a>, with Gera as capital. That state survived until 1918 and became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/peoples-state-of-reuss">People's State of Reuss</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 1647 CE – 1848 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:92.48%;width:4.00%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1647 CE – 1848 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Schleiz</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Heinrich LXII</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Reuß-Schleiz</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1748&amp;focus=principality-of-reuss-schleiz" aria-label="Principality of Reuss-Schleiz on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4949 421 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Reuss-Schleiz lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5269 677L5275 669"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5275 669h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5269 677h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5269 677h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Schleiz. <a href="/atlas?year=1748&amp;focus=principality-of-reuss-schleiz">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
